  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCooler temperatures continue today, with 850mb temperatures ranging \nfrom roughly -5 Celsius to -10 Celsius. These temperatures around \nthe 25th percentile for this time of year, which leads to below \nnormal highs this afternoon in the upper 20s to low 30s. Height \nrises come on Tuesday and kick off a warming trend through the week. \nBy late this week, highs will return to 10-15 degrees above normal \ndue to the continued push of an upper-level ridge moving into the \nregion. \n\nSignal for snow chances Tuesday night into Wednesday morning has \nincreased over the past 24 hours. An upper-level low moves into the \nregion in that time frame, and will be the driving force behind the \nprecipitation potential. NBM guidance now resolves a roughly 20-40% \nchance for a tenth of an inch of snowfall or more over northeastern \nSouth Dakota and western Minnesota through Wednesday morning. The \nevent has only just come into the REFS time range at this point, \nwhich shows a bit more confidence, a roughly 40-60% chance to see \nmeasurable snow through that same time frame. Soundings do show a \nlayer of drier air in the lowest ~1km of the atmosphere, so that is \nlikely where the remaining uncertainty is coming from. Given the \nfairly shallow depth of the dry layer as well as dew point \ndepressions only in the ~5-10 degrees Celsius range, forecaster \nconfidence would lean towards enough saturation of the profile to \nsee snowfall at the surface. Given warm ground temperatures, \nsnowfall may struggles to accumulate, but elevated and grassy \nsurfaces may see light accumulations of a couple tenths of an inch \nat the most. High temperatures Wednesday will reach above freezing \nacross the forecast area, so any snow that does stick will likely \nmelt quickly. Overall impacts are expected to be low. \n\nThe only other chances for precipitation in the forecast come from a \npotential end-of-week system. Ensemble clusters show around an 80% \nchance for a pattern that would support divergence aloft over the \nNorthern Plains. This would bring additional chances for rain and/or \nsnow to the region, and currently the highest chances (10-30%) are \nonce again located over northeastern South Dakota and western \nMinnesota. Still a lot of time for things to change, and overall \nimpacts from this system will likely be limited. A higher potential \nfor impactful weather will be strong westerly winds Friday, \nassociated with a tight pressure gradient from the low pressure \ncenter created by the aforementioned divergence aloft. Current NBM \nprobabilities for reaching 45+ miles per hour reach between 20-40% \nwest of the Missouri River. Given the time range these probabilities \ncould still change significantly over the next couple of days, but \nit is worth keeping an eye on moving forward.",
